## Naive Bayes Algorithm Characteristics
- The Naive Bayes algorithm is based on Bayes' theorem and performs classification by calculating posterior probabilities; its core assumption is feature conditional inde-pendence.
- In the current dataset, there is statistical correlation among features, which violates the independence assumption and leads to biased probability estimates.
- GaussianNB: Assumes features follow a normal distribution; however, the distribu-tion of features in the current dataset may differ from the Gaussian distribution, af-fecting the accuracy of likelihood estimates;
- BernoulliNB: Suitable for binary features; binarizing numerical features in the current dataset may result in the loss of continuous information, thereby reducing classifica-tion accuracy;
- MultinomialNB: Suitable for discrete count features; it is applied approximately to the current data through standardization, but the assumption regarding feature dis-tribution may not hold;
- ComplementNB: An improved variant of MultinomialNB that addresses class im-balance through compensatory weights, but remains constrained by the independence assumption;
- The algorithm has a computational complexity of O(n_features × n_classes). It trains quickly but is sensitive to feature correlations, and its performance is limited on com-plex datasets

