## SVM Algorithm Characteristics
- SVM Binary Classification Performance: Binary classification predictions based on the maximum margin classifier, using kernel techniques to handle non-linearly sepa-rable data
- Linear Separability of Binary Classification Data: The data distribution in feature space exhibits partially linear separability, requiring a kernel function to handle non-linear boundaries
- Impact of Feature Redundancy: Feature noise influences the selection of support vec-tors; SVM automatically handles feature redundancy through its support vector mechanism
- Sensitivity to Regularization: Adjusting the C parameter directly affects the number of support vectors and the complexity of the decision boundary, significantly impact-ing the model’s generalization performance
- Prediction Stability for Borderline Samples: The decision boundary based on support vectors exhibits statistical stability in predictions near borderline samples
- Training-validation discrepancy: The difference between the model’s generalization error and training error, as evaluated via cross-validation, is relatively small;
- Hyperparameter optimization effectiveness: Grid search can identify the optimal combination of regularization and kernel parameters within the parameter space;
- Computational efficiency: The SVM training process, based on quadratic program-ming optimization, exhibits polynomial time complexity in binary classification tasks
- Cross-validation performance: 5-fold cross-validation evaluates the model’s general-ization ability and verifies performance consistency across different data subsets
